%% SECTION Agile and Hybrid Delivery
@@
Which Agile Manifesto principle is reflected when a team delivers working features every two weeks?
A) Deliver working software frequently, preferring the shorter timescale
B) Build projects around motivated individuals and trust them to get the job done
C) Business people and developers must work together daily throughout the project
D) Agile processes promote sustainable development at a constant pace indefinitely
= A
? Frequent delivery of working software in short timescales is a core manifesto principle.

@@
What is the primary measure of progress according to the Agile Manifesto principles?
A) Working software (a working product)
B) Percentage of documentation completed
C) Hours worked by the team
D) Number of meetings held
= A
? Working software is the primary measure of progress.

@@
The developers want to change their workflow for the next sprint. Which event is the most appropriate place to agree on the change?
A) Sprint retrospective
B) Sprint review
C) Daily scrum
D) Backlog refinement
= A
? The retrospective inspects the team's process and plans improvements.

@@
During sprint planning, who decides how many backlog items the developers can complete in the sprint?
A) The developers
B) The product owner
C) The scrum master
D) The project sponsor
= A
? Developers assess their capacity and decide how much they can take on. The product owner clarifies priorities and value.

@@
A product owner is rarely available, and the team is waiting for decisions. What should the scrum master do first?
A) Discuss the impact with the product owner and agree how to ensure availability
B) Make the product decisions on the product owner's behalf to keep the team moving
C) Ask the developers to assume the requirements and correct them in a later sprint
D) Cancel the sprint and restart once the product owner has more time available
= A
? The scrum master coaches the PO and works to resolve the impediment; taking over the PO's decisions is inappropriate.

@@
Which artifact contains the sprint goal, the selected items and the plan to deliver them?
A) Sprint backlog
B) Product backlog
C) Increment
D) Release plan
= A
? The sprint backlog includes the sprint goal (its commitment), the selected product backlog items and the plan.

@@
What is the commitment associated with the increment in Scrum?
A) Definition of done
B) Product goal
C) Sprint goal
D) Velocity target
= A
? Each Scrum artifact has a commitment: product backlog—product goal; sprint backlog—sprint goal; increment—definition of done.

@@
A Kanban board shows 9 items in the "Review" column, which has a WIP limit of 4. What should the team do?
A) Stop starting new work and swarm to clear the review bottleneck
B) Raise the review column's WIP limit to 10 to reflect the current reality
C) Start more development work so that no developer is left idle
D) Remove the review column and let developers review their own work
= A
? Respecting WIP limits means finishing work before starting more; the team collaborates to clear the bottleneck.

@@
Which XP practice ensures that code from all developers is merged and tested frequently, often several times a day?
A) Continuous integration
B) Collective code ownership
C) Planning game
D) Sustainable pace
= A
? Continuous integration merges and tests code frequently to detect integration problems early.

@@
In SAFe, what is the name of the regular event where all teams on an agile release train plan together?
A) PI (program increment) planning
B) Sprint review
C) Scrum of scrums
D) Bidder conference
= A
? PI planning is a face-to-face (or virtual) event where teams on an ART align on objectives for the next increment.

@@
A team's velocity has been 30, 32 and 31. A manager asks the team to commit to 45 points next sprint. What should the scrum master do?
A) Explain that velocity reflects sustainable capacity, and involve the product owner in scope
B) Agree to the target and ask the team to work overtime until all of the extra points are delivered
C) Re-estimate the stories with larger point values so that the team reaches 45 points
D) Relax the definition of done for one sprint so the team can complete more stories
= A
? Velocity is a planning tool, not a target. Forcing higher commitments damages quality, sustainability and trust.

@@
What is the main purpose of the sprint review?
A) Inspect the increment with stakeholders and adapt the product backlog
B) Evaluate each developer's individual contribution to the work done during the sprint
C) Approve the budget for the next sprint with the project sponsor
D) Agree on improvements to the team's way of working and tools
= A
? The sprint review gathers feedback on the product and adjusts the backlog; the retrospective handles process improvements.

@@ | multi
Which two are values of Scrum? (Choose two.)
A) Courage
B) Openness
C) Hierarchy
D) Documentation
E) Competition
= A, B
? The Scrum values are commitment, focus, openness, respect and courage.

@@
A team using Scrum notices that stories often remain "almost done" at the end of the sprint. Which action best addresses this?
A) Limit WIP and slice stories smaller so they can be finished within the sprint
B) Count the "almost done" stories toward velocity to reflect the team's real effort
C) Extend the sprint by a few days whenever stories are close to completion
D) Remove testing from the definition of done so that stories can be closed faster
= A
? Smaller stories and lower WIP improve flow and completion. Extending sprints or counting unfinished work hides problems.

@@
Which agile contract option allows the customer to stop the project when enough value has been delivered, typically by paying a predefined fee?
A) Early cancellation option
B) Graduated fixed price
C) Not-to-exceed time and materials
D) Firm fixed price for the entire scope
= A
? The early cancellation option lets the customer end the contract early, usually with a cancellation fee, when sufficient value has been delivered.

@@
In a hybrid project, the software team uses sprints while procurement of hardware follows a fixed contract with delivery milestones. What is the project manager's key integration responsibility?
A) Align release plans with hardware milestones and manage the dependencies between them
B) Ask the software team to stop using sprints so that both parts follow one schedule
C) Let both parts run independently, and integrate them only at the very end of the project
D) Ask the hardware vendor to deliver in two-week sprints to match the software team
= A
? Hybrid integration aligns plans at integration points and manages cross-component dependencies and risks.

@@
What is the role of the project manager in an agile team according to modern practice?
A) Servant leader who facilitates, removes impediments and coordinates with the organisation
B) Coordinator who assigns the daily tasks to developers to keep the sprint plan on track at all times
C) Decision-maker who orders the product backlog on behalf of the stakeholders
D) Technical lead who decides the architecture and design for the development team
= A
? Agile project managers serve and enable the team; product decisions belong to the product owner and technical decisions to the team.

@@
Which Lean waste is reduced by keeping the same team together across projects?
A) Relearning and handoffs
B) Overproduction only
C) Transportation of materials
D) Extra processing of invoices
= A
? Stable teams retain knowledge, reducing relearning and the loss that occurs during handoffs.

@@
What does a definition of ready help the team achieve?
A) Make sure backlog items are clear and small enough before they enter a sprint
B) Decide when an increment is ready to be released to production users
C) Measure customer satisfaction with each feature after it has been released to the users
D) Approve change requests before they are added to the product backlog
= A
? A DoR sets criteria (clear acceptance criteria, small size, dependencies resolved) for items to be pulled into a sprint.

@@
A stakeholder asks why the agile team won't commit to the full feature list for a fixed release date. What is the best explanation?
A) With a fixed date and team, scope is flexible, so the most valuable features come first
B) Agile teams avoid commitments of any kind, so no feature list can ever be promised
C) The team prefers not to work under pressure, so it keeps its commitments small
D) The release date will be moved automatically until all of the listed features have been completed
= A
? Agile planning often fixes time and resources and varies scope, delivering the highest value first.

@@
Which approach coordinates multiple Scrum teams through a regular meeting of representatives who discuss dependencies and impediments?
A) Scrum of scrums
B) Daily scrum of one team
C) Sprint retrospective
D) Product box exercise
= A
? A scrum of scrums brings team representatives together to coordinate integration and remove cross-team impediments.

@@
In Kanban, which statement about roles and iterations is correct?
A) Kanban prescribes no roles or fixed iterations and starts from the current process
B) Kanban requires a scrum master and fixed two-week iterations to manage the flow of work
C) Kanban forbids work-in-progress limits, because they reduce team utilisation
D) Kanban requires a product owner to accept each card before it can be moved
= A
? Kanban is evolutionary: visualise work, limit WIP, manage flow, make policies explicit and improve collaboratively.

@@ | match
Match each agile concept with its description.
1) Spike
2) MVP
3) Information radiator
4) Velocity
A) Timeboxed experiment to reduce uncertainty
B) Smallest product used to validate a key assumption with users
C) Highly visible display of project information
D) Amount of work completed per iteration
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Spikes reduce uncertainty, MVPs validate value hypotheses, information radiators make information visible and velocity measures completed work per iteration.

@@
Which agile practice best embodies "simplicity — maximising the amount of work not done"?
A) Building only what the current highest-priority needs require
B) Including every possible feature in the first release to avoid later changes
C) Writing complete documentation up front so nothing has to be reworked
D) Designing for all imaginable future scenarios to keep options open
= A
? Simplicity avoids unnecessary work, such as speculative features and premature design.
